# CHEATSHEET-2.un
The re-derivation — nailing the terms that are very unlikely to change (2026-08-08)

**Register**: working sheet, steward-led re-derivation from the problem domain (supersedes CHEATSHEET.un's seating where they conflict; canon segments not yet touched). Guideline in force: millennia of mapping/travel/search practice are the demand map for vocabulary — coinages where an ancient word exists is a smell.

NOTE: Most terms also define several super-and-sub-terms. This format makes it direct.
As an exemplar, the PATH definition below defines 'PATH' but also defines:
  - origin      (== path-origin      == origin-location      == path-origin-location )
  - destination (== path-destination == destination-location == path-destination-location )
  - step        (== path-step )
So a total of 4 concepts/terms with at least 9 total synonyms for various of them.

`@[TERM]`  --> Reference the term directly -- will probably become @<{TERM}> when it's its own value later... that's what we're here to decide
`@{TERM}`  --> For text, leaves it in place but indicates it has (or should have) a definition
**TERM**   --> Bold the first time the term or subterm is used *in its own definition*.
TERM       --> The term or subterm being defined, after the first bolded instance

## Well Settled

|t[DESIGNATOR][MINT][DANGLE][COLLIDE] A **DESIGNATOR** is a name deliberately bound (**MINTED**) to a thing — the binding, not any property of the thing, is the whole connection. Whoever minted it (or inherits its upkeep) keeps the binding; a designator can therefore **DANGLE** (the binding is lost or was never known here) or **COLLIDE** (two mints of one name in one scope). Compare @{DESCRIPTION}: satisfied by the thing's properties, recomputed at every use, never minted.
  :examples [filename slug bibkey "Joseph" UUID]
  ; note the deliberate lean-ness: no "community" dependency — "whoever minted it or
  ; inherits its upkeep" carries the binding-maintenance idea without a new term.
  ; Uniqueness-scoping is NOT here: it lives in LOCATION (unique within parent), because
  ; bare designators (UUIDs) may be unscoped. Scoped-ness is what LOCATION adds.

|t[LOCATION][PARENT-][SUB-] A **LOCATION** is a @{DESIGNATOR} that is unique within its **PARENT-LOCATION**'s scope, and contains any number of inner **SUB-LOCATIONS**.
  :synonyms [place coordinates]
  :examples [city state zip street subdomain directory]
  |rel :parent-location (@[LOCATION] | @[WORLD]) {1,1}
       :sub-locations    @[LOCATION]             {0,N}

|t[WORLD] A root @{LOCATION} that has no (nil) parent. Every LOCATION is transitively within exactly one WORLD, so two locations are addressable-between only when they share one. There are many worlds (the postal world, a filesystem, DNS, one document's tree) — an @{ADDRESS} lives entirely inside one; crossing between worlds is never an address step (it is @{DIRECTIONS} territory — a resolver hop, e.g. a scheme).
  :examples ["/" "the DNS root" "a document root" "the postal world"]
  ; kept community-free per steward note. The one-world-per-location claim is the
  ; load-bearing addition: it's what makes "shared container" well-defined and
  ; absolute-vs-relative meaningful. If a real counter-case exists (a location in
  ; two worlds), this is where the model bends — flagged as its falsifier.

|t[PATH][-ORIGIN-][-DESTINATION-][-STEP][WAYPOINT] A **PATH** is an ordered set of steps between **ORIGIN** @{LOCATION} and **DESTINATION** @{LOCATION}; its intermediate steps are **WAYPOINT**s (== path-step == step).
  Here we scope the DESTINATION to one that attempts to determine or partially determine a specific @{TARGET-SET}.
  DESTINATION is almost always something more specific than @{WORLD}.
  :synonyms [route checkpoints]
  |rel :origin      @[LOCATION] {1,1}
       :destination @[LOCATION] {1,1}
       :step        @[LOCATION] {0,N}
  ; the step question, resolved (coord — revert if it misses):
  ; (a) steps stay type-LOCATION. A "coordinate with no children" is already a legal
  ;     LOCATION (sub-locations {0,N} includes zero) — leaf locations need no new kind.
  ; (b) WAYPOINT is the ROLE a location plays as an intermediate step — the ancient word,
  ;     un-minting "intermediate-location". Origin/destination/waypoint = the three roles,
  ;     all type-LOCATION (the kind/role split doing its job).
  ; (c) address-as-step is COMPOSITION, not a third step-kind: paths concatenate, so a
  ;     sub-PATH (incl. an ADDRESS) given as a step FLATTENS into its locations. Allowing
  ;     it in the written form is sugar; the model's steps stay locations — no muddying.

|t[ADDRESS] An **ADDRESS** is two or more progressively scoped @{LOCATION}s.
  Usually so that the first step after the origin is discernible by which container is shared with the origin.
  |rel :step @[LOCATION] {2,N}
  ; {2,N} confirmed: one location alone is just a LOCATION (and one bare name alone is
  ; just a DESIGNATOR) — address-ness begins when scoping composes. Consistent test:
  ; "Joseph" isn't an address; "Joseph, 123 North St" is.

|t[DIRECTIONS]  PATH that isn't an ADDRESS.
  *Acceptable*: INSTRUCTIONS

## Falls out free (derived, not stipulated)

- **ADDRESS ⊂ PATH** — by DIRECTIONS' complement definition.
- **Address uniqueness is compositional**: each step unique-in-its-parent (LOCATION's definition) ⟹ the whole chain unique from the shared container. Not a separate axiom.
- **Relative and absolute are ONE definition**: absolute address = the case where the shared container is the community root ("/", "USA", the DNS root). No second concept needed.
- **D8 transfers whole to LOCATION**: collision = two mints in one parent; dangle = the parent's community forgot. Scoped natural keys, as always practiced.
- **The subset-sequence law lands home**: an address is declarative (order derivable from scoping, both designation-and-route at once); DIRECTIONS are the imperative remainder (resolver steps, offsets — origin-bound, order essential).

## Un-minted by this sheet

- **containment-coordinate** — LOCATION now holds that seat under its ancient name.
- **universal-path** — likely absorbed: "absolute address" (shared container = the WORLD). Pending confirmation.
- **location-as-contained-in-predicate** (D12's seating) — the contents-of reading needs no term; "within X" is prose.
- **intermediate-location** — WAYPOINT holds that seat under its ancient name (a role of LOCATION in a PATH).
- **naming-community** (as a dependency of the core four) — DESIGNATOR now carries binding-upkeep without it ("whoever minted it or inherits its upkeep"); the term may still earn its own entry later for policing/collision machinery, but nothing here depends on it.

## Coord flags (open, deliberately not resolved here)

- **TARGET-SET** — newly referenced in PATH's usage note, undefined: presumably "the @{ENTITY}s / @{VALUE-OBJECT}s the destination is meant to determine." Needs its nail (and it's where the referent side re-enters this sheet).
- **Endpoint vs occupant** (last round's result, still standing): an ADDRESS designates the endpoint uniquely; it reaches the referent only via what occupies it — the wrong-Joseph seam. Where identity components / IDENTIFIER sit relative to these four is the next nail.
- **The promises** re-derive naturally against these (the-address-promised-permanent / current-best-address / directions-from-here / identifier) — not yet re-stated.
- **REFERENCE / REFERENCE-ACT** (artifact/event — steward-ratified in dialogue) join once the four above settle.
